 "title": "Why Charging Less Makes Patients More Expensive",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator claims that lowering prices in healthcare attracts demanding patients who want discounts and free extra services, leading to lower profits and higher stress. He advises stopping price competition and instead competing on value, building better clinical service, and charging higher prices to attract the right patients.",
 "key_points": [
  "Lowering prices attracts more patients, but more patients do not always mean more profit.",
  "Competing on cheap prices attracts people who choose you mainly because you are cheap.",
  "Low-paying patients want more discounts, question every charge, and expect extra service for free.",
  "Some low-paying patients expect you to do everything they say just because they pay you.",
  "Low-paying patients pay the least but demand the most, causing you and your team to spend more time, energy, and handle more complaints for shrinking profit.",
  "A cheap price does not mean a cheap cost.",
  "Stop competing on price and start competing on value.",
  "Build a better clinical service, deliver a better patient experience, and have the confidence to charge what your care is worth.",
  "Charge higher because the goal is not to attract everyone, but to attract the patients who value your care."
 ],
 "content": "Instagram reel/post by Angus Poh Yang Jun｜Healthcare Coaching. Text / caption: The Cheaper you charge, The more expensive your patient is. #entrepreneurship #drangus #chiropractor #doctor #physiotherapy. Transcript: The cheaper you charge, the more expensive your patient will become. Sounds crazy, right? But this is one of the biggest mistake I see in healthcare business owner. They lower their price and think that they will attract more patient. And yes, they do. But here's the problem. More patient doesn't always means more profit. And if you don't understand this, you can be fully booked and still struggling to make money. When you compete using cheap pricing, you attract people who choose you mainly because you're cheap. They want more discount, they question every single charges, they expect every extra service for free. Some even think, I pay you so you must do everything I say. See, terrible, right? They pay you the least, but demand the most. And guess what? You spend more time, more energy, and your teams will handle more complaint, but the profit getting smaller, smaller and smaller. So who actually pay the price? You are. So here's what I want you to understand. A cheap price doesn't means a cheap cost. So stop competing on price, start competing on value. Build a better clinical service, deliver a better patient experience and have the confidence to charge what your care is worth. Basically mean, charge higher. Because the goal is not to attract everyone. The goal is to attract the patient who value your care. Follow me if you want to learn more about healthcare.",
